 "segments": [
  "[   0.00-   0.60]  Quick question.",
  "[   1.48-   3.60]  How many times have you opened a recruitment email thread",
  "[   3.60-   6.72]  and thought, okay, what's the latest here?",
  "[   7.42-  10.20]  Because in search, the inbox isn't just email.",
  "[  10.84-  12.06]  It's the whole project.",
  "[  12.78-  14.90]  You've got client updates, candidate replies,",
  "[  15.66-  17.20]  scheduling changes, feedback loops,",
  "[  17.58-  18.96]  salary notes, interview logistics,",
  "[  19.90-  24.78]  all buried inside long threads with re, re, re everywhere.",
  "[  25.94-  27.98]  And the real work isn't reading one email.",
  "[  27.98-  30.30]  It's reconstructing the timeline,",
  "[  31.06-  32.32]  figuring out what's been decided",
  "[  33.02-  34.24]  and what needs to happen next.",
  "[  35.10-  36.24]  And that's where things slip.",
  "[  37.04-  39.62]  A client asks for an updated shortlist and it gets missed.",
  "[  40.26-  43.44]  A candidate replies with availability and nobody confirms.",
  "[  44.12-  46.42]  A stakeholder gives feedback and it doesn't get logged.",
  "[  47.04-  48.40]  Not because anyone's careless,",
  "[  48.78-  51.02]  because it's too easy to lose the next action",
  "[  51.02-  51.94]  in a wall of text.",
  "[  53.18-  53.96]  Here's the cleaner way.",
  "[  54.60-  56.56]  The workflow watches your search inbox",
  "[  56.56-  59.52]  and processes threads at the conversation level,",
  "[  59.92-  61.30]  not just individual messages.",
  "[  62.30-  63.58]  When something changes in a thread,",
  "[  64.04-  65.36]  it creates a concise summary.",
  "[  65.94-  69.00]  What's new, what's been decided, and what's still pending.",
  "[  69.84-  72.92]  Then it pulls out next actions like schedule interview,",
  "[  73.56-  77.06]  send CV, request feedback, confirm availability,",
  "[  77.62-  79.72]  and turns them into a simple task list",
  "[  79.72-  81.66]  with owners and due dates.",
  "[  82.42-  85.66]  No rewriting, no manual copy paste into a tracker.",
  "[  86.26-  88.30]  It can also draft the next reply for you.",
  "[  88.80-  91.58]  Short, professional, and grounded in the thread context.",
  "[  92.08-  94.32]  So you're not starting from a blank screen every time.",
  "[  95.02-  96.48]  But it doesn't send anything blindly.",
  "[  97.26-  98.28]  You stay in control.",
  "[  98.84- 101.54]  If the AI is uncertain, like mixed intent,",
  "[ 101.72- 103.84]  conflicting dates, or unclear feedback,",
  "[ 104.38- 106.78]  it flags the thread for human review instead of guessing.",
  "[ 107.70- 109.98]  You approve, edit, or reject.",
  "[ 110.72- 113.38]  And everything is logged so you have a clear audit trail",
  "[ 113.38- 115.54]  of what the workflow summarized and why.",
  "[ 116.58- 117.32]  And this is important.",
  "[ 117.92- 119.84]  It's job-related and evidence-based.",
  "[ 120.56- 122.68]  It summarizes what's actually in the thread.",
  "[ 123.28- 125.58]  If something isn't stated, it doesn't invent it.",
  "[ 126.04- 128.38]  Quick estimate, illustrative assumptions only.",
  "[ 128.98- 131.48]  If it takes about three minutes to read, interpret,",
  "[ 131.96- 133.60]  and update notes for a busy thread,",
  "[ 134.20- 136.60]  and this reduces that to about one minute of review,",
  "[ 137.16- 139.22]  that's about two minutes saved per thread.",
  "[ 140.10- 143.52]  At 200 threads a month, that's roughly 400 minutes,",
  "[ 144.08- 145.76]  around 6.7 hours saved.",
  "[ 146.26- 148.70]  Again, assumptions, not a guaranteed result.",
  "[ 149.70- 151.24]  Independent portfolio prototype.",
  "[ 151.96- 155.42]  Fictional data, results vary, but the benefit is real.",
  "[ 156.02- 158.40]  Fewer missed next steps, cleaner handoffs,",
  "[ 158.86- 161.50]  and an inbox that behaves like an organized search system,",
  "[ 161.86- 162.78]  not a memory test."
